{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789477402,"nonce":"IQIvc/dzKmZ7SD4Bm3qyrGnFiX8=","title":"Python: a string literal boxed in a loop was a box and a copy per iteration","message":"`row[\"flag\"]`, `{\"id\": i, ...}` and every other string literal that flows into a dynamic slot went through `zb_box_str` at the use: a 48-byte box and a copy of the bytes each time the use ran. bench_records made eight of its sixteen allocations a row that way (four literal keys per dict literal) and two more per lookup in its filter loop. The bytes are immutable and no box of them is distinguishable from another.\n\nFix: `const_boxes` (crates/compiler/src/const_boxes.rs), run before the optimisers in the tiered compile. A call that boxes a string constant global (`zb_box_str`, `zyntax_box_str`, `$IO$string_to_dynamic`) becomes a load of a per-constant global; `__zyntax_box_constants` makes the boxes once behind a flag and every entry function calls it first. The globals are writable so the collector roots them; a load is not a site, so the release analysis never frees a shared box. Language-independent: ZynML's `zyntax_box_str` sites take the same path.\n\nbench_records exec 392 to about 350 ms (its build stage 0.41 to 0.34 s wall). The remaining literal boxing is per program, not per iteration.","files":null}]}